1. **Create a `RootObjectMapper`** with the default settings you asked for  
   – dynamic date formats, dynamic templates, date detection, and numeric detection.  
2. **Build an `XContentBuilder`** (the “builder” that will hold the JSON).  
3. **Prepare the serialization parameters** (`includeDefaults` = true, `skipRuntimeFields` = true).  
4. **Call the helper function** `RootObjectMapper_doXContent` that you supplied, passing the builder and the params as *string representations* (the function expects the arguments in that form).

```java
import org.elasticsearch.common.xcontent.XContentBuilder;
import org.elasticsearch.common.xcontent.XContentFactory;
import org.elasticsearch.index.mapper.RootObjectMapper;
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;

// 1. Build the RootObjectMapper with the requested defaults
RootObjectMapper rootMapper = new RootObjectMapper.Builder("root")
        // Enable the default dynamic date formats
        .dynamicDateFormats(true)
        // Enable the default dynamic templates
        .dynamicTemplates(true)
        // Turn on date detection
        .dateDetection(true)
        // Turn on numeric detection
        .numericDetection(true)
        .build();

// 2. Create an XContentBuilder (JSON) that will receive the serialized mapper
XContentBuilder builder = XContentFactory.jsonBuilder();

// 3. Prepare the serialization parameters
//    The function expects a Java ArrayList represented as a string.
//    The first element is `includeDefaults`, the second is `skipRuntimeFields`.
List<Object> params = new ArrayList<>();
params.add(Boolean.TRUE);   // includeDefaults
params.add(Boolean.TRUE);   // skipRuntimeFields

// 4. Call the helper function
//    The function signature is:
//      RootObjectMapper_doXContent({ builder: string, params: string })
Object xcontent = functions.RootObjectMapper_doXContent(new java.util.HashMap<String, Object>() {{
    put("builder", builder.toString());   // string representation of the builder
    put("params", params.toString());     // string representation of the ArrayList
}});

// 5. Inspect the result (if the helper returns the raw JSON string)
System.out.println("Serialized RootObjectMapper:");
System.out.println(xcontent);
```

### What each part does

| Step | Purpose | Key lines |
|------|---------|-----------|
| **1** | Build the mapper with the defaults you want | `dynamicDateFormats(true)`, `dynamicTemplates(true)`, `dateDetection(true)`, `numericDetection(true)` |
| **2** | Create the JSON writer | `XContentFactory.jsonBuilder()` |
| **3** | Tell the serializer to include defaults and skip runtime fields | `params.add(Boolean.TRUE)` twice |
| **4** | Invoke the helper function | `functions.RootObjectMapper_doXContent(...)` |
| **5** | Print the resulting JSON | `System.out.println(xcontent)` |

> **Note**  
> The helper function you provided expects the *string* representation of the builder and the params list.  
> `builder.toString()` gives you the JSON that has been built so far (empty at this point).  
> `params.toString()` yields something like `"[true, true]"`, which the helper interprets as “include defaults” and “skip runtime fields”.

Running this snippet will give you the XContent (JSON) representation of your `RootObjectMapper` with all the requested defaults applied, while runtime fields are omitted.

